  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 138 PM CDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nThe cold front will continue to sag south of the forecast area this \nafternoon. Widely dry weather is in store for the remainder of the \nday across central and southeastern IL. Many people won't see\nsignificant relief from the warmer temperatures until after the\nsystem exits tomorrow night. \n\nThere could be some light, scattered showers late this evening in \nthe west, but the next efficient push of rain will come late tonight \ninto early tomorrow morning as the front pushes back north as a warm \nfront. The morning round of widespread rain could have some high \nrainfall rates at times and be a proficient rain producer. PWATs \nwill be around 1.8-2.1 inches, which is high for the month of \nOctober. The average PWAT for October 15th is around 0.6-0.7 inches \nbased off the SPC sounding climatology. The good news is that our \nsoils can handle this rain decently since we have been so dry \nlately. The rain is welcomed. The LPMM is showing some localized \nswaths of 1-3 inches of rain from 08z Thursday to 08z Friday on\ntop of what has already fallen this morning. \n\nA short period of dry weather will move in briefly early in the \nafternoon tomorrow before the trailing cold front races eastward. \nWhen the cold front advances east tomorrow afternoon/evening, a \nsevere weather risk and additional rain will be accompanying \nit. Currently, SPC has a marginal (level 1 of 5) risk for severe\nweather, including a 5% damaging wind risk and 2% tornado risk,\nfrom about a Pittsfield to Gibson City line and south. The \nenvironment looks supportive of strong to severe thunderstorms. \nStorm type looks like it may begin as discrete, supercells that \nwill evolve into a multicell/QLCS mode as it exits to the east. \nHow much of this primed environment we will achieve will be \ndetermined by if we can recover fully from the morning \nconvection/rainfall. Timing of these storms seems to be \n~20z-02z. After these storms exit to the east, additional \nshowers and thunderstorms (not severe) could occur through \nFriday evening. After Friday afternoon, the forecast will be dry\nthrough the extended period.\n\nAfter the cold front pushes off to our east early Friday morning, \ncooler, more fall like temperatures will be ushered in for a few \ndays. Highs Friday and Saturday will be in the upper 50s to low 60s, \nwith lows in the mid to upper 30s. There is a chance for some patchy \nfrost to develop across the northern half of the state Saturday \nmorning and most of the state Sunday morning. How widespread the \nfrost can get on Saturday morning will depend if the wind doesn't \nget too gusty. Beyond Sunday morning, temperatures will begin \nto warm back up to low 70s for highs on Monday, then to upper \n70s by Wednesday next week. \n\nThe pressure gradient with the passing low pressure system will \ntighten behind the cold front. Wind gusts on Friday will be around \n30-40 mph, with the greatest wind gusts possible west of I-57. The \nLREF shows a 40-70% chance of wind gusts greater than 35 mph west of \nI-57 Friday morning, with gusts relaxing after 1pm that\nafternoon.",
